-- lib/kernel.cub — the smallest machine, written in the language (part of machine-in-hyperactive).
-- The paper's §4.5 for functions, sharing, blocked terms and quotation. A wire is a slot of the
-- memory: empty, holding a value, holding an operation, or holding the two that met there. Every
-- request a step makes is a change to one wire (NkCh). nk-rule is every rule, a function of the two
-- cells that met and the first wire name it may use. nk-plan is a step at one wire: its rule, or a
-- read of a held wire (nothing held yet, nothing asked: waiting is not a step). The executor
-- (NkExec) is outside: it chooses which wire steps next and hands out names. Each statement is made
-- once, over what it is about: a change (nk-ch), the parts of a value (nk-parts), whether a value
-- is blocked (nk-blocked), the held wire a pair reads (nk-held).
-- It loads after the library: the proofs use its lemmas on word equality, and the C experiment
-- that emits it is the library's.
import "library.cub"

